  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "The Thames flooded this tunnel six times before it opened. Rotherhithe to Wapping, started 1825, four hundred metres of brick under a tidal river.",
        "visual_query": "victorian brick tunnel thames london",
        "visual_fallback": "old brick tunnel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody had tunnelled under a navigable river before. The bed was soft silt, dig in and the river poured through. Six men died in 1828.",
        "visual_query": "thames river london water surface",
        "visual_fallback": "murky river water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Marc Brunel's answer was patented in 1818: an iron shield, divided into cells, one miner per cell, brickwork laid right behind as it crept forward.",
        "visual_query": "iron tunnelling shield engineering drawing",
        "visual_fallback": "antique engineering drawing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Engineers long credited the idea to a dockyard pest: the shipworm, which bores through ship timber behind a hard shell, lining the hole as it goes.",
        "visual_query": "wooden ship hull timber dockyard",
        "visual_fallback": "old ship timber"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It worked. Two million people a year paid a penny to walk under the Thames. Overground trains still run through Brunel's brick tube.",
        "visual_query": "london overground train brick tunnel",
        "visual_fallback": "train tunnel entrance"
      }
    ],
