        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Marc Brunel patented the tunnelling shield in January 1818; it was divided into compartments with one worker per cell and the brickwork was placed behind it as it advanced.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "94% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The shield's design is widely credited to the shipworm Teredo navalis, which bores through ship timber behind a hard shell and lines its burrow as it goes.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "The attribution itself is what the narration asserts, and it is well documented: the Brunel Museum records that Brunel saw Teredo navalis damage at Chatham Dockyard and says the inspiration is 'often suggested', and the Conchological Society traces the account to the naturalist J. G. Wood and draws the shell-lined-burrow parallel. The underlying inspiration is traditional, not established by any statement of Brunel's, and Wikipedia's Thames Tunnel article does not mention it at all, which is why the narration says only that engineers have long credited it.",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "claude-research",
        "source": "https://thebrunelmuseum.com/in-defence-of-pests-terredo-navalis/"
      },
